Watch the absolute wording "every": exam statements often sound plausible until a single scope word turns an otherwise familiar idea into an overclaim.

Because the decisive detail is wrong, mark the statement false.', 'TRUE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is correct. In retail fashion markets, a business that prioritises a bakery emphasising recipe quality before surveying breakfast preferences illustrates product orientation starting from internal specifications.

This statement draws on product orientation versus market orientation. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

In this setting, product orientation in retail fashion begins with internal product focus rather than leading with customer feedback revealed preference for smaller portion sizes. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'TRUE — This statement is correct. When customer feedback revealed preference for smaller portion sizes in retail fashion, a market-oriented rival may adapt its offer sooner than a specification-led competitor.

Here you must apply ideas from product orientation versus market orientation to a concrete situation rather than recite a definition from memory. Ownership, customer type, stakeholder group, or country of production usually decides the verdict.

